# Glassdoor through HasData

Two tools. `hasdata_glassdoor_listing_getJobListings` takes `keyword` and `location` and returns thirty postings a page. `hasdata_glassdoor_job_getJobDetails` takes the posting `url` and returns that one posting in full.

The listing already carries the salary, the employer rating, the skills and the age of the posting, so most comp and hiring questions are answerable without opening anything.

## The salary field is two different things

`salary.source` reads either `EMPLOYER_PROVIDED` or `ESTIMATED`. The first is a figure the employer published, the second is Glassdoor's own model for that role and market. Averaging across both produces a number that describes neither, so split on `source` before aggregating and name which set a published figure came from.

`min`, `median` and `max` can all be equal, which is a single stated salary rather than a broken range. `salary` can also be absent, and `period` can read `HOURLY`, so converting to an annual figure is an assumption you are making rather than something the response did.

## What only the detail call has

The listing `description` is a snippet and it ends mid-sentence. Full text, stated qualifications and the `expired` flag live on the detail tool, and a listing page still shows postings the employer has closed. Reading every posting in full costs one call each, so open the few that matter rather than the page.

## Paging and domains

`pagination.otherPages` maps a page number to an opaque token, and that token is what `nextPageToken` takes. Keep the other parameters unchanged while paging.

`domain` selects one of the regional Glassdoor sites, and the US site is the default rather than a value you can pass.

Salary figures and employer ratings are Glassdoor's own numbers. Attribute them instead of presenting them as the market.
